{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Fuel Cell Reversible Voltage.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Fuel Cell Reversible Voltage.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"fuel cell reversible voltage fuel cell reversible voltage","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Fuel Cell Reversible Voltage.md","fileName":"Fuel Cell Reversible Voltage.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Fuel Cell Reversible Voltage","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 22:37\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Fuel Cell Technologies]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Fuel Cell Technologies","Fuel Cell","Gibbs Free Energy","directenergyconversiontechnologies.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Fuel Cell Reversible Voltage.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Fuel Cell Reversible Voltage.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"fuel cell reversible voltage fuel cell reversible voltage","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Fuel Cell Reversible Voltage.md","fileName":"Fuel Cell Reversible Voltage.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Fuel Cell Reversible Voltage","heading":"Fuel Cell Reversible Voltage","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Reversible voltage is the ideal electromotive force if a [[Fuel Cell]] converts the reaction's available work without internal losses. The source computes it as $E=-\\Delta G/(nF)$, where $\\Delta G$ is [[Gibbs Free Energy]] change per mole, $n$ is transferred electrons per mole, and $F$ is Faraday's constant. For its room-temperature hydrogen-oxygen example, the ideal voltage is about 1.23 V; operating voltage falls below it.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Fuel Cell Technologies","Fuel Cell","Gibbs Free Energy","directenergyconversiontechnologies.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Fuel Cell Reversible Voltage.md::2","sourceKey"